{message: Alcohol reaches the brain and temporarily changes how nerve cells communicate. One important effect is that it strengthens the action of **GABA**, a chemical messenger that slows some brain activity. It also reduces the action of **glutamate**, which normally helps excite brain activity. Together, these changes can make some people feel less alert, less inhibited, or briefly less tense.

The feeling is not the same as alcohol removing the cause of stress. It can also make thinking, judgment, coordination, and self-control worse. As the alcohol wears off, a person may feel tired or more anxious, and repeated drinking can lead to dependence and other serious health problems.

So when a soldier’s letter says a drink made them feel calmer, it tells historians about that person’s experience—not that alcohol was a safe or dependable treatment for stress. The comfort of sharing a drink with companions may also have contributed. Alcohol is especially risky for children and young teenagers, whose brains and bodies are still developing.}

