A simple way to describe alcohol’s short-term brain effects is that it can strengthen some **“brake” signals** (involving GABA) and weaken some **“accelerator” signals** (involving glutamate). This makes parts of the brain less active. For some adults, worries may seem less urgent, self-consciousness may ease, and they may feel less tense. Alcohol does not selectively switch off stress, though: it also interferes with judgment, memory, coordination, and self-control. People react differently, and the calmer feeling can be followed by tiredness or increased anxiety.

For troop morale, any brief sense of ease was only one small part of the picture. Reliable food, rest, safety, companionship, and trust in leaders mattered much more to soldiers’ ability to cope over time. Alcohol carries particular risks for children and teenagers, whose brains are still developing.}

